UNIT f64_canonical_bits
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@b15b56613f9d cli/sir/canon.mjs — encode(f64fromBits(raw), f64).bits, the sir-canonical-v1 NaN rule applied to a raw IEEE-754 binary64 bit pattern
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG f64_canonical_bits(raw: text) -> text
ENVELOPE any text. In scope: exactly 16 characters, each one of 0-9 a-f (lowercase ASCII hex), read big-endian as the 64 bits
  of an IEEE-754 binary64 value. Every other text throws (R1): empty, shorter or longer, uppercase or mixed case, any sign,
  prefix, separator, whitespace (including a trailing line feed) or non-ASCII digit.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION The canonical bit pattern of the binary64 value whose raw bits are given: the same 16 lowercase hex digits, except
  that every NaN (either sign, any payload, quiet or signalling) becomes the one canonical quiet NaN. This is the f64 equality
  key of sir-canonical-v1: after it, f64 equality is bit equality, NaN equals NaN, -0 differs from +0, and the infinities are
  distinct.
BEHAVIOR
R1 If raw is not exactly 16 characters each drawn from 0123456789abcdef -> throws. Nothing is trimmed, case-folded, or prefixed
  away; the check is over the whole text, so a trailing line feed after 16 valid digits throws.
R2 Read raw as an unsigned 64-bit integer, most significant digit first. Bit 63 is the sign, bits 62..52 the exponent, bits
  51..0 the fraction.
R3 The value is a NaN exactly when the exponent bits are all ones and the fraction is not zero. A NaN -> the text QNAN, whatever
  its sign bit, its quiet bit (bit 51), or its other payload bits.
R4 Every other pattern -> raw itself, unchanged. This covers +0 and -0 (distinct), +Infinity and -Infinity (exponent all ones,
  fraction zero: NOT a NaN), subnormals, and normals: reading a non-NaN pattern as binary64 and writing it back is the identity.
PINNED text QNAN = "7ff8000000000000" CITED R3 — sign 0, exponent all ones, only the quiet bit set; the sir-canonical-v1 NaN
  (canon.mjs line 3 and QNAN line 8)
INVARIANT the result is always 16 lowercase hex digits; the result is either raw or QNAN; applying the unit to its own result
  returns the result.
ORACLE value -> oracles/f64_canonical_bits.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 54b806de34211cec500e02ff127f77985b57951f4451bfceb36d04a813a73d98
SCOPE in-contract: the 16-hex validation (R1) and the NaN canonicalization (R3, R4). The thrown message is a display hint and
  not graded: only that R1 throws is part of the contract. Out of contract: the encoder's display field (the shortest decimal
  rendering of the value), which encode also returns but this unit does not expose.
PROVENANCE rederive@b15b56613f9d cli/sir/canon.mjs (f64fromBits line 12, f64bits line 9, encode case f64 line 108), driven by
  selfhost/seams/f64_canonical_bits.mjs; companion to f64_bits (selfhost FINDINGS #1).
